[0.005, "o", "\u001b[1mTexas customers are not underspending—they actually spend 6.1% more per customer than Californians \u001b[0m \r\n\u001b[1m($125.97 vs. $118.74); the total revenue gap is entirely driven by having 32% fewer customers.\u001b[0m      \r\n\r\n\u001b[36m                                                                         \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36mMetric\u001b[0m\u001b[1m              \u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36mCalifornia\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36mTexas\u001b[0m\u001b[1m      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36mVariance (TX vs. CA)\u001b[0m\u001b[1m   \u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mTotal Net Revenue\u001b[0m    \u001b[36m \u001b[0m$292,572.91 \u001b[36m \u001b[0m$211,132.76 \u001b[36m \u001b[0m-27.8% (-$81,440.15)   \u001b[36m \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mPurchasing Customers\u001b[0m \u001b[36m \u001b[0m2,464       \u001b[36m \u001b[0m1,676       \u001b[36m \u001b[0m-32.0% (-788 customers)\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mRegister"]
[0.000, "o", "ed User Base\u001b[0m \u001b[36m \u001b[0m3,696       \u001b[36m \u001b[0m2,481       \u001b[36m \u001b[0m-32.9% (-1,215 users)  \u001b[36m \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mSpend per Customer\u001b[0m   \u001b[36m \u001b[0m$118.74     \u001b[36m \u001b[0m$125.97     \u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1m+6.1% (+$7.23)\u001b[0m         \u001b[36m \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mOrders per Customer\u001b[0m  \u001b[36m \u001b[0m1.41        \u001b[36m \u001b[0m1.44        \u001b[36m \u001b[0m+2.1% (+0.03 orders)   \u001b[36m \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mAverage Item Value\u001b[0m   \u001b[36m \u001b[0m$59.05      \u001b[36m \u001b[0m$60.90      \u001b[36m \u001b[0m+3.1% (+$1.85)         \u001b[36m \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mItems per Order\u001b[0m      \u001b[36m \u001b[0m1.43        \u001b[36m \u001b[0m1.43        \u001b[36m \u001b[0mFlat                   \u001b[36m \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36m                                                                         \u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[3m(Note: Net revenue excludes cancelled and returned items across all time. Metric decomposition: \u001b[0m    \r\n\u001b[3mSpend per Customer = Orders per Customer × Items per Order × Avg Item Value.)\u001b[0m                       \r\n\r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mThe premise contradicts customer behavior:\u001b[0m When decomposed into ord"]
[0.000, "o", "ers per customer (1.44 vs.    \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0m1.41) and average item price ($60.90 vs. $59.05), an individual Texan buyer generates more       \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mlifetime revenue than an individual Californian buyer.                                           \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mThe true driver is customer acquisition volume:\u001b[0m California generates $81.4k more total revenue   \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0msolely because it has 1,215 more registered accounts (3,696 vs. 2,481), matching the broader     \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mstate population disparity; conversion rates from sign-up to purchase are essentially identical  \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0m(TX: 67.6% vs. CA: 66.7%).                                                                       \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mActionable next step:\u001b[0m Regional marketing should treat Texas as a high-yield customer acquisition \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mopportunity rather than an under-monetized basket problem, reallocating top-of-funnel acquisition\r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mad spend toward Texas where customer value is proven higher.                     "]
