[0.054, "o", "\r\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⠋\u001b[0m \u001b[2mquery_data\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[?25h\r\u001b[1A\u001b[2K"]
[0.001, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "\u001b[1mTexas customers are not underspending—individual buyers in Texas actually outspend those in \u001b[0m        \r\n\u001b[1mCalifornia, and the aggregate revenue shortfall is entirely driven by having 32% fewer customers.\u001b[0m   \r\n\r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mThe premise is contradicted on a per-customer basis:\u001b[0m Average spend per purchasing customer in    \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mTexas is \u001b[1m$125.97\u001b[0m, which is 6.1% higher than California's \u001b[1m$118.74\u001b[0m (net of returns and             \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mcancellations across 1,676 Texas vs. 2,464 California buyers).                                   \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mUnit economics show Texas leads across all purchasing dimensions:\u001b[0m Texas buyers average slightly  \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mmore orders per customer (1.44 vs. 1.41), match basket size (1.43 items per order), and purchase \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mhigher-value items on average ($60.90 per item vs. $59.05).                                      \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mThe real gap is top-of-funnel customer volume:\u001b[0m Total net revenue in Texas is lower "]
[0.000, "o", "($211,133 vs. \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0m$292,573 in California) solely because Texas has 33% fewer registered users (2,481 vs. 3,696),   \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mreflecting state population differences rather than poor regional engagement.                    \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mConversion and retention behaviors are nearly identical:\u001b[0m Registration-to-buyer conversion is     \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mslightly stronger in Texas (67.6% vs. 66.7%), while return rates (9.6% vs. 10.2%) and            \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mcancellation rates (14.8% vs. 14.0%) show no signs of buyer friction.                            \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mCategory and acquisition channel mix remain stable:\u001b[0m Product category demand tracks evenly across \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mboth markets—Outerwear & Coats (13.6% in TX vs. 12.3% in CA) and Jeans (10.8% in TX vs. 11.4% in \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mCA) lead both states—while Search represents ~70% of total revenue in each region.               \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mCaveats to bear in mind:\u001b[0m Comparisons reflect all-time cumulative transaction data f"]
[0.000, "o", "or active     \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mregistered users; because the demographic pool scales with real-world state population, aggregate\r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mregional metrics will naturally skew toward California unless normalized per customer.           \r\n\u001b[1m • \u001b[0m\u001b[1mRecommended next step:\u001b[0m Reframe regional targets from gross revenue to customer acquisition—audit \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mTexas-targeted search and display ad spend to capture additional buyer volume where customer     \r\n\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mlifetime value is already outperforming California.                                              \r\n"]
